              "raw_responses": [
                "{\"score\":7.0,\"reasoning\":\"Snapshots show physically plausible growth from small early domains to large intermediate domains and a nearly single-phase late state with thermal speckles, broadly matching the reference. Boundaries are lattice-rough but show no obvious unphysical artifacts. However, reversed chronological order and a much smaller early-time panel obscure the progression. Different intermediate times limit coarsening-rate comparison, and the empty L(t) plot prevents verification despite the similar stated exponent. Tiny labels, weak curve contrast, and missing curve legends substantially reduce visualization quality.\"}",
                "{\"score\":7.0,\"reasoning\":\"Snapshots show physically plausible coarsening from small early domains to large intermediate regions and a nearly single-phase late state with residual thermal speckles, consistent with the reference up to spin inversion. Boundaries are lattice-like without obvious unphysical artifacts. However, reversed chronological ordering, an undersized early panel, and different intermediate sampling obscure growth-rate comparison. Final domain size is qualitatively similar, but the empty growth plot, tiny labels, and poorly identified diagnostic curves substantially weaken visualization quality and quantitative support.\"}",
                "{\"score\": 7.5, \"reasoning\": \"Snapshots show physically plausible coarsening from small early domains to large intermediate domains and a nearly uniform late phase with residual thermal speckles, consistent with the reference up to spin inversion. Boundaries are lattice-rough but lack obvious unphysical artifacts. Final ordering is similar, though different intermediate times and reversed panel order impede coarsening-rate comparison. Visualization quality is substantially weaker: tiny labels, unequal snapshot sizes, an empty growth plot, and unclear auxiliary curves.\"}"
